.\" Assisted-by: Claude Code:claude-sonnet-5-5
.Dd July 1, 2026
.Dt JOTAIN-DEEP-RESEARCH-REVIEW 7
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm jotain-deep-research-review
.Nd deep-research review — implementation & docs (2026-07)
.Sh DESCRIPTION
A close review of the Jotain Elisp layer and its documentation, cross-checked against \fBupstream Emacs 30/31 behaviour\fP and a set of recent Emacs writing.
Findings only reflect upstream Emacs semantics; anything that would only hold on the
.Ql "github:jylhis/emacs"
Meson fork was deliberately ignored.
.Sh METHOD
.Bl -bullet -compact
.It
\fBRead\fP every
.Ql "lisp/init-*.el" ,
.Ql "early-init.el" ,
.Ql "init.el" ,
and the affected
.Ql "docs/*.mdx"
pages.
.It
\fBCompared\fP the requested themes (spell-check, mode line, docs-as-service, diary, structured navigation) against the source material listed under Sources.
.It
\fBVerified\fP each candidate finding adversarially (refute-by-default) before it was allowed into this report.
One plausible-looking bug (an alleged
.Ql "vundo-unicode-symbols"
void-variable at load time) was \fBrefuted\fP —
.Ql "vundo-unicode-symbols"
is an autoloaded
.Ql "defconst" ,
so the eager
.Ql ":custom"
is correct — and is \fInot\fP listed below.
.El
.Pp
Because no Emacs binary is available in this environment (Nix dev-shell only), Elisp semantics were checked by reasoning plus the repo's own
.Ql ".claude/skills/{elisp-dev,emacs-internals}"
reference notes, not by execution.
Line numbers are against the tree at review time.
.Sh SUMMARY OF FINDINGS
.Bd -literal -offset indent
| # | Sev | Area | File | What |
| 1 | Med | Docs / Info | lisp/init-docs.el:36 | add-to-list 'Info-directory-list before info-initialize hides every built-in manual in the fallback path |
| 2 | Med | Project | lisp/init-project.el:127 | compile-multi entry keyed on python-mode never fires (buffers are python-ts-mode); %file-name% is not substituted |
| 3 | Low | Core / internals | early-init.el:22 | bidi-display-reordering nil is an unsupported debug-only knob and redundant |
| 4 | Low | Editing | lisp/init-editing.el:89 | (use-package newcomment …) is defined twice, byte-identical |
| 5 | Low | Spell | lisp/init-writing.el:52 | (org-mode . jinx-mode) is redundant — org-mode derives from text-mode |
| 6 | Low | Org / state | lisp/init-org.el:53,83 | org-clock-persist-file and org-roam-db-location litter outside var/ |
| 7 | Low | Keys | lisp/init-keys.el:60 | jotain-keyboard-quit-dwim has a dead cond branch; docstring says "deactivate" but calls keyboard-quit |
| 8 | Low | Devops | lisp/init-lang-devops.el:28 | setq used on the dockerfile-mode-command defcustom (repo rule: setopt) |
| 9 | Low | Docs drift | lisp/init-devenv.el:35 | @doc still claims devenv-reload refreshes "through envrc" after direnv was dropped |
| 10 | Low | Docs drift | docs/keybindings.mdx | Missing C-c j/C-c M-j (majutsu), global C-c C-e (eca), C-x C-g/C-x C-j; C-c C-d miscategorised as mode-local |
| 11 | Info | Spell | lisp/init-writing.el:51 | jinx never runs in prog-mode; code comments/docstrings go unchecked |
| 12 | Info | Diary | lisp/init-org.el | No appt, diary-file, or org-agenda-to-appt — no pre-event reminders |
| 13 | Info | Core / GC | lisp/init-core.el:38,48 | 16 MiB GC threshold is a magic number duplicated; minibuffer-exit reset can drift and fires under nested minibuffers |
| 14 | Info | Docs | docs/finding-information-in-emacs.mdx:6 | "AI generated" banner is warranted; one illustrative symbol-file return value is wrong |
.Ed
.Pp
No high-severity or security issues were found.
The configuration is unusually clean; most items below are polish, consistency, and doc-drift.
.Sh 1. SPELL-CHECKING — JINX (REQUESTED AREA)
.Ql "lisp/init-writing.el"
already uses \fBjinx\fP (enchant-backed, JIT font-lock driven) rather than flyspell, which is exactly the direction of the emacsredux \(dqReplacing Flyspell with Jinx\(dq https://emacsredux.com/blog/2026/07/13/replacing-flyspell-with-jinx/ post.
Two refinements:
.Pp
\fB1a — Redundant Org hook (Finding 5, low).\fP The
.Ql ":hook"
is:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
:hook ((text-mode . jinx-mode)
       (org-mode  . jinx-mode))
.Ed
.Pp
.Ql "org-mode"
is
.Ql "define-derived-mode org-mode outline-mode" ,
and
.Ql "outline-mode"
is derived from
.Ql "text-mode" .
.Ql "define-derived-mode"
runs the parent modes under
.Ql "delay-mode-hooks"
and then
.Ql "run-mode-hooks" ,
which runs the delayed
.Ql "text-mode-hook"
(and
.Ql "outline-mode-hook" )
in every Org buffer.
So
.Ql "(text-mode . jinx-mode)"
\fBalready\fP enables jinx in Org; the
.Ql "org-mode"
entry just toggles the (idempotent) minor mode a second time. → Drop line 52.
.Pp
\fB1b — No prog-mode coverage (Finding 11, enhancement).\fP The whole reason the emacsredux post recommends
.No "\fB" Ns Ql "global-jinx-mode" Ns No "\fP"
over per-mode hooks is that jinx decides \fIwhat\fP to check by \fBface\fP: in a programming buffer it limits itself to comments and strings automatically, so one mode replaces both
.Ql "flyspell-mode"
and
.Ql "flyspell-prog-mode" .
Jotain's per-mode hooks mean typos in code comments and docstrings are never flagged.
This may be intentional; if comment/docstring checking is wanted, either switch to
.Ql "global-jinx-mode"
or add
.Ql "(prog-mode . jinx-mode)" .
The current bindings
.No \&( Ns Ql "M-$"
→
.Ql "jinx-correct" ,
.Ql "C-M-$"
→
.Ql "jinx-languages" )
already match the post exactly, and disabling
.Ql "text-mode-ispell-word-completion"
(to avoid the NixOS \(dqno word-list\(dq corfu error) is a correct, well-reasoned touch.
.Sh 2. MODE LINE & UI (REQUESTED AREA)
.Ql "lisp/init-ui.el"
uses \fBdoom-modeline\fP
.No \&( Ns Ql "doom-modeline-height 28" ,
LSP segment on, GitHub/encoding off).
Compared to davep's minimalist mode-line post https://blog.davep.org/2026/06/15/more-mode-line-tweaking.html (which pares a
.Ql "mood-line"
down to buffer/project/VC), doom-modeline is a heavier, batteries-included choice — a legitimate philosophical difference, not a defect.
Worth noting only as a design tension: doom-modeline already renders project/VC/LSP, so if the config ever trends toward minimalism,
.Ql "mood-line"
or a hand-rolled
.Ql "mode-line-format"
would be the lighter path.
\fBNo change recommended.\fP The rest of the module (font-preference
.Ql "cl-loop"
with
.Ql "find-font"
probing, emoji fontset fallback,
.Ql "load-theme"
disable-others advice, the
.Ql "calendar-intermonth-text"
propertize \fIform\fP — correctly an evaluated sexp, not a pre-computed string) is correct.
.Sh 3. DOCS-AS-SERVICE & INFO DISCOVERABILITY (REQUESTED AREA)
Framed against Charles Choi's \(dqIn Emacs, everything looks like a service\(dq http://yummymelon.com/devnull/in-emacs-everything-looks-like-a-service.html: the post's real thesis is \fImodel every integration as a client/server with a UI layer, a client edge, and a local store\fP — and Jotain's
.Ql "devenv.el"
transient +
.Ql "@doc"
cookie convention + bundled Info manual is a good expression of \(dqmake the service discoverable.\(dq
But the discoverability layer has a real bug and some drift.
.Pp
\fB3a — Info fallback hides the standard manuals (Finding 1, medium).\fP
.Ql "lisp/init-docs.el:36" :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
(add-to-list 'Info-directory-list found)
.Ed
.Pp
.Ql "Info-directory-list"
defaults to
.Ql "nil"
and is populated \fBlazily\fP by
.Ql "info-initialize"
(from
.Ql "INFOPATH"
+
.Ql "Info-default-directory-list" ),
which only runs
.Ql "(unless Info-directory-list …)" .
Pre-seeding it here with a single directory means
.Ql "info-initialize"
later sees a non-nil value and does nothing — so in the source-checkout fallback path the Info reader can find
.Ql "jotain.info"
\fBbut no longer finds the Emacs, Elisp, Org, or any other bundled manual.\fP That defeats the module's own purpose.
Fix: add to
.No "\fB" Ns Ql "Info-additional-directory-list" Ns No "\fP"
instead (designed for user additions, searched \fIafter\fP the standard set, never suppresses initialisation), or call
.Ql "(info-initialize)"
immediately before the
.Ql "add-to-list" .
(The Nix-wrapped build is unaffected — it uses
.Ql "INFOPATH" ,
which
.Ql "info-initialize"
reads correctly — so this only bites the exact path the module exists to serve.)
.Pp
\fB3b —
.Ql "docs/keybindings.mdx"
drift (Finding 10, low).\fP The reference table has fallen behind
.Ql "init-*.el" :
.Bl -bullet -compact
.It
.Ql "C-c j"
→
.Ql "majutsu-log"
and
.Ql "C-c M-j"
→
.Ql "majutsu-dispatch"
.No \&( Ns Ql "init-vc.el:182" ,
labelled \(dqjujutsu\(dq in
.Ql "init-keys.el" )
are \fBmissing\fP from the
.Ql "C-c"
table.
.It
Global
.Ql "C-c C-e"
→
.Ql "eca"
.No \&( Ns Ql "init-ai.el:51" )
is undocumented; the page only lists the dired-local
.Ql "C-c C-e"
→
.Ql "wdired"
override, without noting the global binding it shadows.
.It
.Ql "C-x C-g"
→
.Ql "jotain-switch-git-status-buffer"
and
.Ql "C-x C-j"
→
.Ql "jotain-switch-jj-status-buffer"
.No \&( Ns Ql "init-vc.el:46,119" )
are \fBmissing\fP from the
.Ql "C-x"
table.
.It
.Ql "C-c C-d"
→
.Ql "helpful-at-point"
is bound \fBglobally\fP in helpful's
.Ql ":bind"
.No \&( Ns Ql "init-help.el:51" ),
not in
.Ql "helpful-mode-map" ;
the page lists it under \(dqMode-local overrides\(dq as a helpful-mode binding.
It should move to the global list, with a note that
.Ql "sops-mode"
locally overrides
.Ql "C-c C-d" .
.El
.Pp
\fB3c — AI-generated docs banner (Finding 14, info).\fP The \(dqAI generated\(dq note on
.Ql "docs/finding-information-in-emacs.mdx"
is appropriate and honest; the page is broadly accurate and repo-consistent.
One illustrative example gives a wrong
.Ql "symbol-file"
return shape — worth a one-line correction to match the
.Ql "locate-library"
example later in the same file — but nothing structural.
.Sh 4. DIARY / CALENDAR / APPOINTMENTS (REQUESTED AREA)
.Ql "lisp/init-ui.el"
configures the built-in \fBcalendar\fP well (ISO week numbers, Monday
.Ql "calendar-week-start-day" ),
and
.Ql "init-org.el"
wires up org-agenda, capture, roam, and clock.
But — motivated by the ray-on-emacs \(dqEmacs Carnival: Diary\(dq https://ray-on-emacs.blogspot.com/2026/06/emacs-carnival-diary-part-2.html post — there is a genuine \fBfunctional gap\fP (Finding 12, enhancement):
.Bl -bullet -compact
.It
No
.Ql "appt"
activation, so \fBno pre-event reminders\fP ever fire for timed entries; org-agenda is an on-demand view only.
.It
No
.Ql "diary-file"
and no
.Ql "org-agenda-include-diary" Ns No "/" Ns Ql "org-agenda-to-appt"
bridge, so free-form and sexp diary entries
.No \&( Ns Ql "diary-float" ,
anniversaries) aren't surfaced anywhere.
.El
.Pp
If reminders are wanted, an
.Ql "appt"
.Ql "use-package"
block
.No \&( Ns Ql ":ensure nil" ,
.Ql "(appt-activate 1)" ,
.Ql "appt-message-warning-time" ,
and
.Ql "(org-agenda-to-appt)"
on
.Ql "org-agenda-finalize-hook"
plus a light idle timer) closes it.
If the on-demand agenda is considered sufficient, this should be recorded as an explicit non-goal — but it is not equivalent to having reminders.
.Sh 5. STRUCTURED NAVIGATION & EDITING (REQUESTED AREA)
Measured against emacsredux's \(dqEssential Structured Navigation and Editing Commands\(dq https://emacsredux.com/blog/2026/06/20/essential-structured-navigation-and-editing-commands/: Jotain correctly leans on the \fBbuilt-in\fP sexp family
.No \&( Ns Ql "C-M-f/b" ,
.Ql "C-M-u/d" ,
.Ql "C-M-a/e" ,
.Ql "C-M-SPC" ,
.Ql "C-M-k" ,
.Ql "C-M-t" )
— which are tree-sitter-aware in Emacs 30+ — rather than re-binding them, and layers
.Ql "expreg"
.No \&( Ns Ql "C-=" ),
.Ql "multiple-cursors" ,
and the prose-level
.Ql "transpose-sentences" Ns No "/" Ns Ql "-paragraphs"
on top.
It also opts into the Emacs 31
.Ql "delete-pair-push-mark"
and
.Ql "kill-region-dwim"
niceties (guarded for Emacs 30).
Two issues:
.Pp
\fB5a — Duplicate
.Ql "newcomment"
block (Finding 4, low).\fP
.Ql "init-editing.el"
defines
.Ql "(use-package newcomment …)"
\fBtwice\fP — lines 54–74 and again 89–109 — byte-for-byte identical, separated only by the
.Ql "emacs"
transpose block.
The second expansion silently re-runs.
Delete lines 89–109.
.Pp
\fB5b — Dead
.Ql "cond"
branch in DWIM quit (Finding 7, low).\fP
.Ql "init-keys.el:57" :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
(cond
 ((> (minibuffer-depth) 0)                     (abort-recursive-edit))
 ((get-buffer-window "*Completions*" 'visible) (delete-completion-window))
 ((region-active-p)                            (keyboard-quit))   ; ← dead
 (t                                            (keyboard-quit)))
.Ed
.Pp
The
.Ql "region-active-p"
branch is identical to the fallback, so it is dead; worse, the docstring promises \(dqRegion active → deactivate it,\(dq which implies
.Ql "(deactivate-mark)" .
Either drop the branch or make it do what the docstring says.
.No \&( Ns Ql "keyboard-quit"
does deactivate the mark as a side effect, so behaviour is fine today — but the code doesn't match its contract.)
The paired global
.Ql "M-s R"
bindings —
.Ql "replace-regexp-as-diff"
(global) vs
.Ql "dired-do-replace-regexp-as-diff"
.No \&( Ns Ql "dired-mode-map" )
— do \fBnot\fP conflict; the buffer-local map shadows the global, and the code comments say so.
.Sh 6. CORE, EARLY-INIT & INTERNALS (EXPANSION VS THE THESIS)
Cross-checked against the attached thesis \fI\(dqThe GNU Emacs Architecture — Unlocking the Core\(dq\fP (Karlsson, Uppsala 2026), which documents the single-threaded command loop, the cooperative \fBGIL\fP-serialised Lisp threads, and stop-the-world mark-and-sweep GC.
The config's performance machinery is \fBconsistent with that model\fP: the
.Ql "early-init"
GC-defer
.No \&( Ns Ql "gc-cons-threshold most-positive-fixnum" ),
the runtime 16 MiB restore + idle
.Ql "garbage-collect" ,
and the minibuffer GC pause are all correct applications of \(dqraise the threshold so
.Ql "consing_until_gc"
is large; there are no threads to race.\(dq
Nothing in the config wrongly assumes
.Ql "make-thread"
buys parallelism.
Two refinements and one correctness nit:
.Pp
\fB6a —
.Ql "bidi-display-reordering nil"
(Finding 3, low).\fP
.Ql "early-init.el:22"
sets it via
.Ql "setq-default" .
Upstream documents this variable as intended \fBonly for debugging\fP; setting it to
.Ql "nil"
is discouraged and can mis-display real RTL text.
It is also \fBredundant\fP:
.Ql "bidi-paragraph-direction 'left-to-right"
+
.Ql "(setopt bidi-inhibit-bpa t)"
are the supported LTR performance knobs — and are exactly the two the repo's \fIown\fP
.Ql ".claude/skills/emacs-internals/references/redisplay.md"
cites as the mechanism (it does \fBnot\fP mention
.Ql "bidi-display-reordering" ).
Drop the
.Ql "bidi-display-reordering nil"
clause; keep the other two.
(Impact is cosmetic and only with RTL text present — hence low, not medium.)
.Pp
\fB6b — GC magic number (Finding 13, info).\fP
.Ql "16 * 1024 * 1024"
appears twice
.No \&( Ns Ql "init-core.el:38"
and the
.Ql "minibuffer-exit-hook"
reset at line 48).
A single
.Ql "(defconst jotain-gc-cons-threshold (* 16 1024 1024))"
referenced from both removes the drift risk.
Separately, the exit hook lowers the threshold unconditionally, even when an \fIouter\fP recursive minibuffer is still open
.No \&( Ns Ql "enable-recursive-minibuffers"
is on) — guarding on
.Ql "(= (minibuffer-depth) 0)"
keeps the high threshold until the outermost minibuffer is dismissed.
Both are minor.
.Pp
\fB6c — Thesis caveats.\fP The briefing flagged two thesis details that are version-stale and should \fInot\fP inform the config: \fBpure space\fP (§7.4.6) was removed in Emacs 30 (pdumper-only), and a
.Ql "max_specpdl_size"
description is inaccurate.
Neither affects Jotain; noted so the internals notes don't inherit them.
.Sh 7. BROADER SWEEP
.Bl -bullet -compact
.It
.No "\fB" Ns Ql "compile-multi"
python key (Finding 2, medium).\fP
.Ql "init-project.el:127"
keys entries on
.Ql "python-mode" ,
but the repo routes tree-sitter modes via
.Ql "major-mode-remap-alist"
(commit \fI\(dqroute ts-modes via major-mode-remap-alist\(dq\fP), so Python files open in
.Ql "python-ts-mode"
and this entry \fBnever appears\fP in the picker.
Go/Nix/Rust/Zig correctly use their
.Ql "-ts-mode"
keys; Python is the odd one out (as are
.Ql "haskell-mode" Ns No "/" Ns Ql "meson-mode" ,
worth an audit).
Also
.Ql "\(dqpytest %file-name%\(dq"
—
.Ql "compile-multi"
does \fBnot\fP template
.Ql "%…%" ;
it would run that literal string.
Use
.Ql "python-ts-mode"
and a function-valued action that builds the argv from
.Ql "buffer-file-name" .
.It
\fBOrg state outside
.Ql "var/"
(Finding 6, low).\fP
.Ql "org-clock-persist"
writes to
.Ql "~/.emacs.d/org-clock-save.el"
and
.Ql "org-roam"
to
.Ql "~/.emacs.d/org-roam.db"
— both land in the repo root under
.Ql "just run" ,
contrary to the config's own
.Ql "var/"
discipline
.No \&( Ns Ql "init-core.el"
themes every other state file).
Set
.Ql "org-clock-persist-file"
and
.Ql "org-roam-db-location"
to
.Ql "(jotain-var-file …)" .
.It
.No "\fB" Ns Ql "setq"
on a defcustom (Finding 8, low).\fP
.Ql "init-lang-devops.el:28"
uses
.Ql "(setq dockerfile-mode-command cmd)"
inside a
.Ql "boundp"
guard; the repo rule is
.Ql "setopt"
for defcustom options (so
.Ql ":set" Ns No "/" Ns Ql ":type"
run).
.Ql "(setopt dockerfile-mode-command cmd)"
with a literal symbol and runtime value is valid here.
.It
.No "\fB" Ns Ql "@doc"
drift (Finding 9, low).\fP
.Ql "init-devenv.el:35"
says
.Ql "devenv-reload"
refreshes \(dqthrough envrc (the direnv substrate configured in init-prog),\(dq but direnv/envrc was dropped for the native devenv env loader (commit \fI\(dqDrive project env via native devenv\(dq\fP); the same
.Ql "@doc"
even refers to the \(dqnative env loader\(dq a few lines later.
Reword to the native loader.
.El
.Sh WHAT'S ALREADY STRONG
Worth stating plainly, since a findings list skews negative: the module boundaries and the \(dqpackage that enhances a built-in shares its file\(dq rule are followed consistently;
.Ql ":ensure nil"
discipline is correct throughout; Emacs-31-only features are uniformly
.Ql "boundp" Ns No "/" Ns Ql "fboundp" Ns No "-guarded"
so the config loads clean on 30; the
.Ql "custom.el" Ns No "-supersession"
advice, the
.Ql "save-place"
recenter, and the
.Ql "savehist"
\(dqwhy
.Ql "register-alist"
is excluded\(dq comment show real care about edge cases; and the
.Ql "@doc"
cookie convention plus bundled Info manual make the config genuinely self-documenting — the spirit of the \(dqeverything is a service\(dq post.
.Sh SUGGESTED SEQUENCING
.Bl -enum -compact
.It
